                       "displayName":  "Identity - Break-Glass Account Sign-in Detected",
                       "enabled":  true,
                       "description":  "DETECTS\nAny sign-in attempt, successful or failed, by an account whose UserPrincipalName starts with \"breakglass\".\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nBreak-glass accounts exist solely as emergency access when normal admin paths fail. There is no legitimate scenario where they sign in during routine operations - every hit is either a scheduled test (expected and announced) or a genuine emergency/compromise (needs immediate attention). This is a rare high-fidelity rule: it should almost never fire, and when it does, the response is immediate investigation, not triage.\n\nTRIAGE\n1. Confirm whether this is an announced quarterly test or unplanned.\n2. If unplanned, check the source IP and location - there is no \"normal\" baseline for this account, so any sign-in is worth understanding.\n3. Check ResultType - a successful sign-in during a genuine emergency confirms the account works; a successful sign-in with no known cause is a compromise investigation.\n4. Verify the account was used only for its intended emergency scope, then confirm the emergency root cause is resolved.\n5. Rotate the account password after every real use, scheduled or not.\n\nFALSE POSITIVES\nNone by design. Scheduled quarterly tests should be announced in advance so the resulting alert is expected, not suppressed.\n\nSOURCE\nSigninLogs",
                       "alertRuleTemplateName":  null
                   }
}